{"parentUuid":"3305094b-a190-40bb-b20e-1456613864ae","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_0166McLBDWwrUoip2UZHKFAZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Toq9j6zE3dHxuvKhCPFfdC","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"CP-SAT\" portfolio worker thread parallel Python overhead"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9091,"cache_read_input_tokens":24650,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9091,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":58,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011Caotp71qwuzUfAuh5qZMc","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bd3a22d2-c010-4226-a2e4-ea0ae164a02b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:09:54.510Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bd3a22d2-c010-4226-a2e4-ea0ae164a02b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_0166McLBDWwrUoip2UZHKFAZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VNKUcnFoKniSTyuraqGBcG","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/cmake/README.md","prompt":"List the supported CMake options, build flags, and any mention of LTO, PGO, march=native, or release optimization. Include the minimum compiler/cmake versions and build commands."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9091,"cache_read_input_tokens":24650,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9091,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":58,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011Caotp71qwuzUfAuh5qZMc","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ec841202-3226-4cc6-9144-8e0bc17fd9d9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:09:56.014Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3305094b-a190-40bb-b20e-1456613864ae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UsnDU436H6LdG2xV9cVu4p","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Clang PGO instrumented build llvm-profdata workflow tutorial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"How To Build Clang and LLVM with Profile-Guided Optimizations — LLVM 23.0.0git documentation\",\"url\":\"https://llvm.org/docs/HowToBuildWithPGO.html\"},{\"title\":\"How To Build Clang and LLVM with Profile-Guided Optimizations — LLVM 10 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://releases.llvm.org/10.0.0/docs/HowToBuildWithPGO.html\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Build Configurations — LLVM 5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://releases.llvm.org/5.0.2/docs/AdvancedBuilds.html\"},{\"title\":\"How To Build Clang and LLVM with Profile-Guided Optimizations — LLVM 19.1.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://releases.llvm.org/19.1.0/docs/HowToBuildWithPGO.html\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Build Configurations — LLVM 23.0.0git documentation\",\"url\":\"https://llvm.org/docs/AdvancedBuilds.html\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Build Configurations — LLVM 9 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://releases.llvm.org/9.0.1/docs/AdvancedBuilds.html\"},{\"title\":\"How To Build Clang and LLVM with Profile-Guided Optimizations — LLVM 9 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://releases.llvm.org/9.0.1/docs/HowToBuildWithPGO.html\"},{\"title\":\"How To Build Clang and LLVM with Profile-Guided Optimizations — LLVM 8 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://releases.llvm.org/8.0.1/docs/HowToBuildWithPGO.html\"},{\"title\":\"Profile-guided Optimization - The rustc book\",\"url\":\"https://doc.rust-lang.org/beta/rustc/profile-guided-optimization.html\"},{\"title\":\"How To Build Clang and LLVM with Profile-Guided Optimizations — LLVM 22.0.0git documentation\",\"url\":\"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/llvm-project/en/latest/LLVM/llvm/html/HowToBuildWithPGO.html\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive search results about Clang PGO instrumented build and llvm-profdata workflow. Here's what I found:\n\n## Overview of the Workflow\n\nPGO (Profile-Guided Optimization) allows your compiler to better optimize code for how it actually runs, and users report that applying this to Clang and LLVM can decrease overall compile time by 20%.\n\n## Key Steps in the Workflow\n\nAt a high level, the way PGO works is that you build an instrumented compiler, then you run the instrumented compiler against sample source files. While the instrumented compiler runs it will output a bunch of files containing performance counters (.profraw files).\n\nAfter generating all the profraw files you use llvm-profdata to merge the files into a single profdata file that you can feed into the LLVM_PROFDATA_FILE option.\n\n## Building the Instrumented Compiler\n\nTo build an instrumented compiler, you need to configure a Clang build with the CMake args: -DLLVM_BUILD_INSTRUMENTED=IR (which causes everything to be built with instrumentation) and -DLLVM_BUILD_RUNTIME=No (because a few projects have bad interactions when built with profiling).\n\n## Merging Profile Data\n\nThe profile merge can be done with /path/to/stage1/llvm-profdata merge -output=/path/to/output/profdata.prof path/to/stage2/profiles/*.profraw.\n\n## Automated Script\n\nLLVM provides a script at utils/collect_and_build_with_pgo.py, tested on a few Linux flavors, which requires a checkout of LLVM, Clang, and compiler-rt. Despite the name, it performs four clean builds of Clang, so it can take a while to run to completion.\n\nThe official LLVM documentation at https://llvm.org/docs/HowToBuildWithPGO.html provides detailed step-by-step instructions if you need more information.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5b906325-3265-46bc-bd2c-f7d1b9d40bc5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:00.771Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3305094b-a190-40bb-b20e-1456613864ae","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ec841202-3226-4cc6-9144-8e0bc17fd9d9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VNKUcnFoKniSTyuraqGBcG","type":"tool_result","content":"# OR-Tools CMake Build Configuration\n\n## Minimum Requirements\n- **CMake**: Version 3.24 or higher\n- **Compiler**: C++20 compatible (GCC 10 or above)\n\n## Key CMake Options\n\n### Build Targets\n| Option | Default | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|---------|\n| `BUILD_CXX` | ON | Compile C++ library |\n| `BUILD_PYTHON` | OFF | Generate Python bindings |\n| `BUILD_JAVA` | OFF | Create Java wrapper |\n| `BUILD_DOTNET` | OFF | Build .NET wrapper |\n| `BUILD_DEPS` | OFF | Compile all dependencies |\n\n### Solver Support\n- **Enabled by default**: CBC, CLP, CP-SAT, GLOP, HiGHS, SCIP\n- **Disabled by default**: CPLEX, GLPK (require explicit `-DUSE_*=ON`)\n- **Toggle options**: `USE_COINOR`, `USE_HIGHS`, `USE_SCIP`, `USE_GLPK`, `USE_CPLEX`\n\n### Build Type & Optimization\nThe document specifies `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` with \"Release\" as default but provides no details regarding LTO, PGO, or `-march=native` flags.\n\n## Build Commands\n\n**Configure with dependencies:**\n```\ncmake -S. -Bbuild -DBUILD_DEPS=ON\n```\n\n**Build:**\n```\ncmake --build build\n```\n\n**View all available options:**\n```\ncmake -S. -Bbuild -LH\n```\n\nNo explicit discussion of profile-guided optimization, link-time optimization, or native architecture targeting appears in this documentation."}]},"uuid":"587c3020-a956-40b9-926b-1e5d5e610011","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:01.646Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ec841202-3226-4cc6-9144-8e0bc17fd9d9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ec36caf0-97d6-4f08-83ac-66bcff9c02f9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NpAbUDeTJPMRk1K6zr7RXn","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"or-tools\" benchmark Linux vs Windows performance CP-SAT\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Linux vs Windows Server Response Time and Throughput Benchmarks Statistics 2026\",\"url\":\"https://commandlinux.com/statistics/linux-vs-windows-server-response-time-and-throughput-benchmarks/\"},{\"title\":\"Is Linux faster than Windows? To be precise, is a program run in Linux faster than that of Windows, given the same hardware specification? - Quora\",\"url\":\"https://www.quora.com/Is-Linux-faster-than-Windows-To-be-precise-is-a-program-run-in-Linux-faster-than-that-of-Windows-given-the-same-hardware-specification\"},{\"title\":\"Linux vs Windows Performance: Real World Dual-Boot Test Results | Windows Forum\",\"url\":\"https://windowsforum.com/threads/linux-vs-windows-performance-real-world-dual-boot-test-results.389100/\"},{\"title\":\"Linux is performing better than Windows 11 according to this benchmark test - gHacks Tech News\",\"url\":\"https://www.ghacks.net/2022/07/09/linux-is-performing-better-than-windows-11-according-to-this-benchmark-test/\"},{\"title\":\"Linux Hardware Reviews & Performance Benchmarks, Open-Source News - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/\"},{\"title\":\"Linux vs. Windows results / Geekbench / Discussion Area - Primate Labs Support\",\"url\":\"https://support.primatelabs.com/discussions/geekbench/17050-linux-vs-windows-results\"},{\"title\":\"Windows 11 Vs. Ubuntu Linux Benchmarks Performance - OpenBenchmarking.org\",\"url\":\"https://openbenchmarking.org/result/2107013-IB-WIN11LINU39\"},{\"title\":\"Comparing Performance on Windows, Linux and OS X\",\"url\":\"https://blog.segger.com/comparing-performance-on-windows-linux-and-os-x/\"},{\"title\":\"Linux vs Windows Performance | Learn Top 5 Most Useful Differences\",\"url\":\"https://www.educba.com/linux-vs-windows-performance/\"},{\"title\":\"Windows vs Linux performance :: Hardware and Operating Systems\",\"url\":\"https://steamcommunity.com/discussions/forum/11/669472753009609444/\"}]\n\nThe search results returned general Linux vs Windows performance benchmarks, but didn't contain specific information about OR-Tools or CP-SAT benchmarks. Let me search more specifically for OR-Tools benchmarks.\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Google vs IBM: A Constraint Solving Challenge on the Job- ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1909.08247\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking your Model - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/08_benchmarking.html\"},{\"title\":\"arXiv:2504.14506v1 [math.OC] 20 Apr 2025 On Solving the Set Covering Problem\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.14506\"},{\"title\":\"Hexaly, CP Optimizer, OR-Tools, Gurobi, Cplex on very large-scale instances of the Job Shop Scheduling Problem (JSSP)\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/benchmarks/hexaly-vs-cp-optimizer-vs-or-tools-on-the-job-shop-scheduling-problem-jssp\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization of Timed Petri Nets using CP-SAT - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405896324000946\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT vs Kalis and CP Optimizer?\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/qnLMdvCZayA\"},{\"title\":\"📈 #76 Optimizing with Google OR-Tools: strategies and insights from three years of real-world experience\",\"url\":\"https://www.feasible.club/p/76-optimizing-with-google-or-tools\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT poor performance\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/tmF6DmQBYU4\"}]\n\nBased on my web search results, I was unable to find specific benchmarks comparing OR-Tools CP-SAT performance between Linux and Windows. The search results show various benchmark comparisons of OR-Tools and CP-SAT with other solvers (like CP Optimizer, Gurobi, and Hexaly) on optimization problems, but they don't contain direct Linux vs Windows performance comparisons for OR-Tools specifically.\n\nThe results do include:\n\n- Comparisons of OR-Tools' CP-SAT solver against other constraint solvers like IBM's CP Optimizer on job-shop scheduling problems\n- Information that CP-SAT is competitive or better than state of the art on academic scheduling benchmarks and better than commercial solvers on small to medium scheduling problems\n- General benchmarking guidance for CP-SAT, but without platform-specific comparisons\n\nIf you're looking for specific Linux vs Windows performance data for OR-Tools CP-SAT, you may need to:\n1. Run your own benchmarks on identical hardware with both operating systems\n2. Check the OR-Tools GitHub repository or discussion forums for any documented performance comparisons\n3. Consult academic papers that specifically test OR-Tools on both platforms\n\nWould you like me to search for anything more specific related to OR-Tools or constraint solver benchmarking?\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"6d559f19-d524-41c4-a8bb-07049248bb1c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:01.708Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ec36caf0-97d6-4f08-83ac-66bcff9c02f9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bd3a22d2-c010-4226-a2e4-ea0ae164a02b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Toq9j6zE3dHxuvKhCPFfdC","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"CP-SAT\" portfolio worker thread parallel Python overhead\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Parallel search problem in CP-SAT solver\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/i3TF_Szuz_k\"},{\"title\":\"Find many CP-SAT solutions, multi-threaded, under time constraint\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/GNgvcMpEwmE\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT multi-thread crashing python w/ large number of workers · Issue #2019 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2019\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: not all threads are used, why? · Issue #2399 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2399\"},{\"title\":\"Bypassing the GIL for Parallel Processing in Python – Real Python\",\"url\":\"https://realpython.com/python-parallel-processing/\"},{\"title\":\"Embarrassingly parallel for loops — joblib 1.6.dev0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://joblib.readthedocs.io/en/latest/parallel.html\"},{\"title\":\"Solver Max - 10 times faster, running cases in parallel\",\"url\":\"https://www.solvermax.com/blog/10-times-faster-running-cases-in-parallel\"},{\"title\":\"concurrent.futures — Launching parallel tasks — Python 3.14 ...\",\"url\":\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/concurrent.futures.html\"},{\"title\":\"How to use ThreadPoolExecutor in Python3 ? - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/how-to-use-threadpoolexecutor-in-python3/\"},{\"title\":\"Python ThreadPoolExecutor: Use Cases for Parallel Processing | by Abdul Rafee Wahab | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://blog.wahab2.com/python-threadpoolexecutor-use-cases-for-parallel-processing-3d5c90fd5634?gi=3969772ad712\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about CP-SAT parallel search with worker threads in Python. Here's what the results show:\n\n## CP-SAT Parallel Search with Worker Threads\n\nThe CP-SAT solver uses the `num_search_workers` parameter to enable multiple threads during a search, where each thread can have different parameters and different random seeds.\n\n### Performance Considerations\n\nTo achieve speedup with multiple threads, you need sufficient CPU cores—otherwise other threads will slow down the single thread that finds a good solution. Some runs with multi-threaded execution can actually perform worse than the single-threaded case.\n\nFor best performance, using 8 threads is recommended. In parallelism, various outcomes are possible including speedup, slowdown, and other behaviors, with large performance gains being the standard behavior in experiments.\n\n### Python-Specific Thread Overhead\n\nFor Python parallel processing more broadly, overhead associated with context switching becomes troublesome when tasks start competing for limited resources. Performance improvement from threading is close to but not exactly linear due to the overhead of creating and managing threads.\n\nAdditionally, if your code can release the GIL (Global Interpreter Lock), using thread-based backends is more efficient because it avoids communication overhead of process-based parallelism, and scientific Python libraries like numpy, scipy, pandas and scikit-learn often release the GIL in performance-critical code paths.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f858e056-e1b0-49f9-9a35-f1bdcaf028de","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:02.080Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bd3a22d2-c010-4226-a2e4-ea0ae164a02b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
