{"parentUuid":"9cc3317f-e4de-4ac5-adc3-e74707cf83d4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01SYu3K6dwzQPC6gRgjWZbpi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PdWvrzPDTWJJhg5vkgYTDa","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.16649","prompt":"Summarize this paper on PGO. Specifically: what speedups are reported, on what types of workloads, what corpus designs were used, is it applicable to branch-heavy code like SAT solvers, and what are the recommended best practices for representative profile collection."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":341,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3120,"cache_read_input_tokens":49050,"output_tokens":326,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3120},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":341,"output_tokens":326,"cache_read_input_tokens":49050,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3120,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaottG6A6NF47huCUeEVG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"62862876-4bf4-4b62-863c-1a8f7c04dfcd","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:52.172Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9cc3317f-e4de-4ac5-adc3-e74707cf83d4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c8cdae05-aaaa-4408-8a9a-59c4a4939d97","agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015oGKjzQmLSR2gMEW8jYj81","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CPython 3.13 PGO LTO build improvement Python benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - faster-cpython/benchmarking-public: A public mirror of our benchmarking runner repository · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/faster-cpython/benchmarking-public\"},{\"title\":\"Usage — Python Performance Benchmark Suite 1.14.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pyperformance.readthedocs.io/usage.html\"},{\"title\":\"Python Speed Center : Comparison\",\"url\":\"https://speed.python.org/comparison/\"},{\"title\":\"Sixteen Years of Python Performance - Lost\",\"url\":\"https://lost.co.nz/articles/sixteen-years-of-python-performance/\"},{\"title\":\"State of Python 3.13 Performance: Free-Threading - CodSpeed\",\"url\":\"https://codspeed.io/blog/state-of-python-3-13-performance-free-threading\"},{\"title\":\"Python 3.14 Is Here. How Fast Is It? - miguelgrinberg.com\",\"url\":\"https://blog.miguelgrinberg.com/post/python-3-14-is-here-how-fast-is-it\"},{\"title\":\"Mailman 3 PGO Optimized PyPy Built - pypy-dev - python.org\",\"url\":\"https://mail.python.org/archives/list/pypy-dev@python.org/thread/3OMFGGOOB37B67P2PYQT3QSBJNKEYP6H/\"},{\"title\":\"Performance of the Python 3.14 tail-call interpreter - Made of Bugs\",\"url\":\"https://blog.nelhage.com/post/cpython-tail-call/\"},{\"title\":\"Timed CPython Compilation Benchmark - OpenBenchmarking.org\",\"url\":\"https://openbenchmarking.org/test/pts/build-python\"},{\"title\":\"The -fprofile-update=atomic flag makes the PGO build way slower on i686 · Issue #148535 · python/cpython\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/148535\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about CPython 3.13 with PGO and LTO build optimizations:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Build Configuration**: CPython can be built with PGO (Profile Guided Optimization) and LTO (Link Time Optimization) using the configure command: `./configure --enable-optimizations --with-lto=yes`.\n\n**Performance Improvements**: Python 3.13 with optional JIT squeezed out additional performance gains beyond Python 3.12. More specifically, a 10% speedup was reproduced \"in the wild\" as compared to Python 3.13 using builds from python-build-standalone.\n\n**Benchmarking Infrastructure**: The faster-cpython project benchmarks multiple builds including: a default PGO build with LTO, a Tier 2 interpreter variant, and a JIT build that combines both PGO and LTO optimizations.\n\n**Python 3.13 Release Focus**: CPython 3.13 was the most performance-oriented release in some time, featuring free-threaded mode with GIL disabled, a new JIT compiler, and the mimalloc allocator bundled by default.\n\nThe search results indicate that PGO and LTO optimizations are standard build configurations used for performance benchmarking CPython, with measurable improvements in execution speed across various benchmarks.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f8821b9f-673b-4362-9a8e-72f527a300f9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:56.553Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9cc3317f-e4de-4ac5-adc3-e74707cf83d4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"1cd3bd74-bc0d-478d-af9f-b63a6910976e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c8cdae05-aaaa-4408-8a9a-59c4a4939d97","agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Vjq6AzK6c2KE5LkmfS3JX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"profile guided optimization\" measured speedup branch heavy compiler benchmark percent\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"From Profiling to Optimization: Unveiling the Profile Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.16649v1\"},{\"title\":\"111 From Profiling to Optimization: Unveiling the Profile Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.16649v1\"},{\"title\":\"Hardware-based Profile Guided Optimization (PGO) from Intel\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/hwpgo.html\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Hardware Counted Profile-Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/268748065_Hardware_Counted_Profile-Guided_Optimization\"},{\"title\":\"GCC Profile Guided Optimizations\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/profile-guided-optimizations-in-gcc\"},{\"title\":\"C++ Series 06 | PGO (Profile-Guided Optimization) | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://yc-kuo.medium.com/c-series-06-pgo-profile-guided-optimization-8a978bd6881f\"},{\"title\":\"How to Build Profile-Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-01-30-profile-guided-optimization/view\"},{\"title\":\"How to Use Profile-Guided Optimization (PGO) on Ubuntu\",\"url\":\"https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-03-02-profile-guided-optimization-pgo-ubuntu/view\"},{\"title\":\"kindatechnical() | Optimizing Compiler Design - Profile-Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://kindatechnical.com/optimizing-compiler-design/profile-guided-optimization.html\"},{\"title\":\"GCC 12 Profile Guided Optimization Benchmarks With The AMD Threadripper 3990X - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/news/GCC-12-PGO-TR-3990X-AMD\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are key findings about Profile Guided Optimization (PGO) measured speedup on branch-heavy compiler benchmarks:\n\n## Overall Speedup Performance\n\nPGO can yield speedups often in the range of 5%-30% on real applications, far surpassing what purely static heuristics can deliver. More specific measurements show:\n\n- Instrumentation: 5–10% on integer workloads, up to 30% in hot kernel loops; LBR Hardware sampling: 3–7% (SPEC), 80–93% of instrumentation's gains on C++\n- 5-20% performance improvement for CPU-bound workloads\n\n## Branch-Specific Results\n\nFor branch-heavy scenarios specifically, one study on branch prediction showed improvements of +13% faster through PGO optimization. This demonstrates PGO's particular effectiveness when branch prediction is the bottleneck.\n\n## Measurement Methods and Profiling Overhead\n\nInstrumentation-based profiling typically incurs 10–50% runtime overhead during profiling, with the mean reported as 16% on SPEC CPU2006; hardware-sampling regimes achieve similar performance improvements at <2% overhead.\n\nThe search results indicate that PGO speedups are highly variable depending on the specific benchmark and whether branch optimization is the primary bottleneck in the application.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"882c5fc9-0716-4ce7-853a-79a231972c8f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:56.608Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1cd3bd74-bc0d-478d-af9f-b63a6910976e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"62862876-4bf4-4b62-863c-1a8f7c04dfcd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c8cdae05-aaaa-4408-8a9a-59c4a4939d97","agentId":"aa2cd8e2e60b20719","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PdWvrzPDTWJJhg5vkgYTDa","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary of PGO Paper\n\nBased on the arXiv abstract provided, I can only offer limited information, as the full paper content isn't included in the webpage excerpt.\n\n## Available Information\n\nThe paper is a **survey** on Profile Guided Optimization that \"systematically categorizes PGO research by profiling method (instrumentation vs. sampling), optimizations (compile time and link/post-link time), compiler integration (GCC, LLVM), and target architectures.\"\n\n## What's Not Available\n\nThe webpage content does not include:\n- **Specific speedup numbers** or performance benchmarks\n- **Workload types** tested\n- **Corpus designs** used for evaluation\n- **Applicability to branch-heavy code** like SAT solvers\n- **Best practices for profile collection**\n\nThe abstract mentions that \"Performance evaluation on representative examples demonstrates PGO's speedups, overheads, and integration maturity,\" but the actual results, examples, and recommendations are contained in the full paper—which is not included in the provided webpage excerpt.\n\n## To Get This Information\n\nYou would need to access the actual PDF or HTML version of the paper (linked on the page) to find the specific performance data, workload details, and best practices you're asking about."}]},"uuid":"25bd70f4-75d9-4ca6-9fc3-2561e1a2bcf7","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:10:57.762Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"62862876-4bf4-4b62-863c-1a8f7c04dfcd","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
